# multipart Memory Deviation Review

This record started with a window in the multipart memory profile. The visible result in the window did not match the original plan; the first thing observed was a deviation on the profile side, not an implementation failure with an established attribution.

The working judgment at the time was to narrow the window first, to avoid mixing the same set of results into later comparisons. This restored the situation to a state where observation could continue, but it did not answer where the deviation entered or whether it occurred only on the multipart path.

The lookup started from `lib/httparty/cookie_hash.rb` and the cookie call name. This entry point was used because the retained state from the request needed to be matched against the redirect transition trace; it was not a conclusion about module ownership. The trace should still be retained, because after the window is closed, it is the only thing that can show at what stage the request state was reinterpreted.

The only sequence that can currently be confirmed is:

1. After the memory profile window started, the results deviated from the plan;
2. Further expansion of the window was paused first;
3. After the temporary restoration, the redirect transition trace was still retained;
4. The cookie-related entry point was included in the review;
5. The cause remains undetermined.

The initial inclination was to attribute the issue to an error in the profile itself. This was only a working hypothesis: it explains why the window was narrowed, but not why the trace still contains a transition that needs another look. The follow-up review should first examine the coexistence of the headers before and after the transition, then decide whether it is worth continuing to investigate through the memory profile.

The impact scope is also being recorded narrowly at this stage. What is known is that the comparison from this window cannot be treated as a stable sample; what is unknown is whether a similar header regression exists and whether the deviation is amplified when cookie state and authorization appear at the same time.

Temporary restoration is not a root-cause fix. Do not delete the redirect transition trace or the cookie lookup entry point from that time just because the window is usable again. We still lack a review result that examines both in the same pass.
